[
    {
        "content": "<p>In <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/31859\">#31859</a>, I introduced a definition of a point of a Grothendieck site and constructed the corresponding fiber functors on (pre)sheaves: the definition of a point was a nontrivial adaptation of SGA 4 IV 6.3 <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/be0a10d1f1d5c9434f47080fa0e2aa48b678ab3a/Mathlib/CategoryTheory/Sites/Point/Basic.lean#L62-L74\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/be0a10d1f1d5c9434f47080fa0e2aa48b678ab3a/Mathlib/CategoryTheory/Sites/Point/Basic.lean#L62-L74</a>. I have been developing this notion (and with Christian Merten, we constructed a conservative family of points for the étale site of a scheme <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/35136\">#35136</a>). In particular, I showed in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/35572\">#35572</a> that when the coefficient category is monoidal (and satisfy certains additional properties), fiber functors are monoidal, and in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/35584\">#35584</a>, this allowed me to give a different proof of the properties that are required in order to show that a category of sheaves is monoidal: it suffices that the site has enough points.</p>",
        "id": 579469819,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1773521754
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I have extended this to (pre)sheaves of modules. In the draft PR <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/35773\">#35773</a>, I give a different approach to the module structure on stalks, by providing a slightly different construction of a colimit of modules over a filtered inductive system of rings, and in the case of commutative rings, I show that this colimit defines a monoidal functor. In particular, the fiber functor on presheaves of modules given by a point of the site is a monoidal functor, and in a similar fashion as I obtained that categories of sheaves with values in a monoidal category tend to be monoidal, I have shown that if the site has enough points, then the category of sheaves of modules over a commutative sheaf of rings is monoidal.</p>",
        "id": 579469825,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1773521762
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In particular, if <code>X</code> is a scheme, the category of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">O</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal O_X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02778em;\">O</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0278em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.07847em;\">X</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>-Modules is monoidal <a href=\"https://github.com/joelriou/mathlib4/blob/presheaf-of-modules-point/Mathlib/AlgebraicGeometry/Modules/Monoidal.lean\">https://github.com/joelriou/mathlib4/blob/presheaf-of-modules-point/Mathlib/AlgebraicGeometry/Modules/Monoidal.lean</a></p>",
        "id": 579469835,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1773521773
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>With some additional efforts, it should be possible to verify that the full subcategory of quasi-coherent sheaves is also monoidal.</p>",
        "id": 579469838,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1773521778
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Aristotle was apparently able to <a href=\"https://aristotle.harmonic.fun/dashboard/requests/fb8471d1-3187-4d39-ad13-6f6f202a481b\">construct the following instances</a> in 700+ lines without introducing points, showing the category of sheaves of modules over a commutative sheaf of rings is monoidal without an \"enough points\" assumption. It's on v4.28.0 though and needs to be cleaned up and bumped before PRing to Mathlib, but we really would like to have the instances ASAP in order to define the Picard group of a scheme (or ringed space).</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">namespace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SheafOfModules</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">SmallCategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">GrothendieckTopology</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Sheaf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CommRingCat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">}}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MonoidalCategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">SheafOfModules</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">sheafCompose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">forget₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">..</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">obj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">SymmetricCategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">SheafOfModules</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">sheafCompose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">J</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">forget₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">..</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">obj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">end</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SheafOfModules</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 616095508,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1786542550
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In any case, with the existing API, what we need to prove is a <code>Prop</code>. The development of the API for points will be useful anyway for the applications. The slightly more direct route is via the internal Hom of (pre)sheaves of modules: <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"1003282\">@Brian Nugent</span> may have made some progress in this direction?<br>\nIn <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/35773\">#35773</a>, I am not very far from proving that the category of quasicoherent sheaves over a scheme is monoidal.</p>",
        "id": 616098422,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1786543309
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For any of this, I do not think mathlib needs Aristotle.</p>",
        "id": 616098634,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1786543364
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I have a draft PR <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40271\">#40271</a> that defines internal Hom but I wasn’t very happy with it and put it on the back burner. If there is interest in defining the Picard group (in particular if the monoidal category structure lands) than I could pick it back up.</p>",
        "id": 616105550,
        "sender_full_name": "Brian Nugent",
        "timestamp": 1786545145
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Also if anyone wants to take over <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40271\">#40271</a>, they are welcome to.</p>",
        "id": 616105663,
        "sender_full_name": "Brian Nugent",
        "timestamp": 1786545173
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Internal homs are definitely important too! It seems the adjunction with tensorLeft is still TODO. Is the idea to construct the MonoidalCategory from the adjunction? Mathlib doesn't seem to have such a constructor yet.</p>",
        "id": 616173134,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1786566223
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The idea would be to use the same strategy as in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/CategoryTheory/Sites/Monoidal.lean\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/CategoryTheory/Sites/Monoidal.lean</a> which is the monoidal category structure for sheaves with values in a monoidal category.</p>",
        "id": 616174486,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1786566694
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It seems the internal homs haven't even been defined there?</p>",
        "id": 616175262,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1786566947
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well, something was defined...</p>",
        "id": 616177124,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1786567568
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I see that <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/CategoryTheory/Localization/Monoidal/Basic.html#CategoryTheory.LocalizedMonoidal\">CategoryTheory.LocalizedMonoidal</a> is used, which is also used by Aristotle ...</p>",
        "id": 616180746,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1786568980
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You may have to look slightly above in the file...</p>",
        "id": 616181065,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1786569119
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42860\">#42860</a> is draft PR for monoidal structure on <code>SheafOfModules</code></p>",
        "id": 616898278,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1786953638
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The strategy of proof is interesting (expressing the tensor product of (pre)sheaves of modules as a quotient of the tensor product of the underlying (pre)sheaves of abelian groups). It looks very much ok to proceed with this, even though the other approaches will also be useful.</p>",
        "id": 616912479,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1786957775
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am not sure this could be useful, but there is a slightly related Lemma <code>ker_mapOfCompatibleSMul</code> in <code>Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/TensorProduct/Quotient.lean</code>, characterizing the kernel <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mo>⊗</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>N</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mo>⊗</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\otimes_R N\\to M\\otimes_A N</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\"><span class=\"mbin\">⊗</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">N</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\"><span class=\"mbin\">⊗</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">A</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">N</span></span></span></span> for an <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span></span></span></span>-algebra <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span>. <br>\nTogether with the surjectivity of this map and setting <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span></span></span></span>, this looks like the non-sheafy version of that statement.</p>",
        "id": 617156022,
        "sender_full_name": "Leonid Ryvkin",
        "timestamp": 1787047191
    }
]